# Round 16 / lane P3 - printed random-number tables + Cicada's own published bytes
#
# REGENERATION
# ------------
# Everything ignored here is fetched or derived and is rebuilt by committed code:
#
#   1) python liber-primus/analysis/round16/P3_tables/build_pads.py
#        -> data/digits.txt, data/pads/*.bin (28 pads), pads_manifest.json,
#           and refreshes corpus_inventory.json's view of the PGP inputs.
#
#      It downloads RAND's *A Million Random Digits with 100,000 Normal Deviates*
#      (MR1418) datafile from
#        https://www.rand.org/content/dam/rand/pubs/monograph_reports/MR1418/MR1418.digits.txt.zip
#      (a Mozilla User-Agent is required; the default urllib UA gets a CloudFront 403),
#      asserts sha256(rand.zip)   == 9083cef4571f3a79729aaf9f4d3cdb946c26033b77b4f929155ca067ad0d8d8b
#          and sha256(digits.txt) == 657622b6eba115bec547ddd83093d259b370294ae6efaa48f5c4e3b3690b862e
#      and that the first printed line is the published 10097 32533 76520 13586...,
#      then assembles the Cicada pads from corpus/A-primary-artifacts/pgp/ (READ ONLY),
#      liber-primus/analysis/pp49_51/canon_256.bin, and the 56 onion7 page JPEGs named
#      by liber-primus/handoff/capsule/MANIFEST.json (images.onion7.p00-p55).
#
#   2) python liber-primus/analysis/round16/P3_tables/sweep.py
#        -> data/partial/<pad>.json (one checkpoint per pad; the run is resumable:
#           re-invoke and it skips pads that already have a checkpoint)
#        -> results.json (collected from every checkpoint)
#      Add --only pad1,pad2 to sweep a subset, --budget SECONDS to bound one invocation.
#      Re-collect without sweeping anything:  python sweep.py --budget 0
#
#   3) python liber-primus/analysis/round16/P3_tables/sweep_ms8.py
#        -> data/partial_ms8/<pad>.json checkpoints -> results_ms8.json
#           (ADDENDUM pass: max_skip=8, `hexchars` replaced by lib_padsweep.ks_nibbles)
#      python liber-primus/analysis/round16/P3_tables/render_addendum.py
#        -> rewrites the '## Addendum' section of RESULTS.md from results_ms8.json
#      data/run_structure.json (longest constant run per pad) is regenerated by the
#      snippet quoted in RESULTS.md's addendum; it is an input to that section only.
#
# COMMITTED (the record of what was actually swept, and self-sufficient without data/):
#   build_pads.py, sweep.py, pads_manifest.json (name/bytes/sha256/source per pad),
#   corpus_inventory.json, results.json, results_ms8.json, render_addendum.py,
#   sweep_ms8.py, RESULTS.md
#
# NOTE: corpus/ is written by another session and is a moving target, so a later rebuild
# can produce different pads. The per-pad sha256 in pads_manifest.json pins the exact
# bytes this lane swept; compare against it before trusting a rebuild.
data/
__pycache__/
smoke_manifest.json
smoke_results.json
